2026-11-19 21:07:07 - Agent_20261119210411 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed the CVE-2024-35195 style TLS verification pooling issue in requests/adapters.py (version 2.30.0, before the fix in 2.32.0).

Submitted findings:
1. TP — requests/adapters.py:338 (get_connection): connection pools are keyed only by URL (select_proxy + prepend_scheme_if_needed + parse_url + connection_from_url) with no regard to the `verify` setting, so a pool created for a `verify=False` request is handed out again for a later `verify=True` request to the same host. Once cert_verify has set CERT_NONE on that pool, subsequent "verified" requests silently proceed with TLS verification disabled, enabling MITM.
2. TP — requests/adapters.py:249-273 (cert_verify): mutates shared, reusable pooled connection state (`conn.cert_reqs`, `conn.ca_certs`, `conn.ca_cert_dir`) at request time; the false branch sets CERT_NONE, and this state persists in the pool beyond the single request.
3. TP — requests/adapters.py:458 (send): calls self.cert_verify(conn, ...) on the connection returned from the shared pool manager, which is what actually applies the poisoned TLS state to a pooled connection that other (verify=True) requests will reuse.
4. FP — requests/adapters.py:173-175 (init_poolmanager): simply constructs urllib3 PoolManager; no verify bypass, configuration only.
5. MR — requests/adapters.py:237-247 (cert_verify docstring/signature region reported by py/tls-verify-debug): the "TLS-related function" hit is informational; the real issue is captured at the body lines (249/273) and the shared-pool mutation, already reported.

Evidence used: grepped for cert_verify callers (only adapters.py:458), cert_reqs assignments (266/273), connection_from_url (351/356), and poolmanager.clear() (366, only on close) confirming the pool is reused across requests with a URL-only key and no change to the key when verification settings differ.
