2026-11-19 21:08:17 - Agent_20261119210411 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed requests==2.30.0 connection-pool/TLS handling (CVE-2024-35195) in requests/adapters.py. Confirmed the root cause across all 4 CodeQL results plus one additional independent location: `get_connection()` (line 330-346) obtains a connection from `self.poolmanager.connection_from_url(url)` keyed on URL alone, and `proxy_manager_for()` (line ~328/350) caches proxy managers in `self.proxy_manager` keyed only by the proxy URL — neither incorporates `verify`/`cert` into the pool key. `send()` (line 455) then calls `cert_verify()` (line 458) which mutates the SHARED pooled connection in place, setting `conn.cert_reqs = "CERT_REQUIRED"` for verify=True paths but falling through to `conn.cert_reqs = "CERT_NONE"`, `conn.ca_certs = None`, `conn.ca_cert_dir = None` (line 273) whenever `verify` is falsy or the URL is non-HTTPS. Because that connection object is returned to the pool and reused for later requests to the same origin, a single `verify=False` request (or a redirect/retry to http://) permanently downgrades TLS verification for all subsequent requests on that Session/pool — enabling MITM of connections the application explicitly asked to verify. `init_poolmanager` (line 155) and `__setstate__`/`init_poolmanager` (line 169) recreate the PoolManager without any per-connection verification state binding, so unpickled or re-initialized adapters retain the same unsafe keying. Submitted 5 individual true positives (adapters.py lines 155, 169, 330, 350, 458) with severity critical/high, CWE-295/CWE-287/CWE-284, and exploit payloads demonstrating the Session-level TLS downgrade.
